# Development guidelines

## General

- Use the GitHub CLI (`gh`) for all GitHub-related tasks when possible
- Be sure to typecheck and lint when you’re done making a series of code changes
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }}
- In a repo whose origin is in the `nordicnaturals` Bitbucket workspace, load the `nordic` skill before naming a branch, committing, or opening a PR. Its conventions (the Jira key as the branch name and at the start of every commit and PR title) override the commit and PR guidelines below
{{- end }}

When importing types in React, prefer `import { type TypeName } from React;` over using `React.TypeName` in the code.

## Communication

Lead with the claim, then give the evidence for it. I have a hard time following a message that walks through evidence first and reaches its conclusion at the end. State the conclusion in the first sentence, then explain why. For example: "Shopify will reject future logins when user IDs on the CI runner change for the same email. Here's why..."

This applies to responses, investigation summaries, commit bodies, PR descriptions, and review comments.

## Writing voice

Commits, PRs, review comments and Jira text go out under my name, so write them the way I would: short and plain. Say why the change exists and anything a reviewer can't see in the diff. Leave the rest to the diff, the issue and the linked thread.

- Most commits need no body. When one does, one or two sentences.
- A PR description is usually its references plus one to three sentences. A small PR can be just a link.
- Use everyday words and short sentences, one idea each. Split a sentence rather than joining clauses with a semicolon or dash.
- Write as me. "I", "we" and "let's" are fine, and so is saying what I'm unsure of ("I couldn't get the form to load locally, so this is untested").
- Keep audit IDs, spec numbers and other identifiers only when a reader needs them to look something up.

